WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:07.630
The last words of Steve Jobs were: “Oh wow,
oh wow, oh wow”.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.900
He died as he lived – filled with wonder.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.840
He had this magical way of thinking

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.830
that you could create the reality that you
envisioned in your head.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.359
That outlook gave him a stunningly successful
life.

00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:24.249
But it may have also contributed to his early
death.

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:30.239
Steve Paul Jobs was born in San Francisco
on February 24, 1955.

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:34.140
According to a cousin, his birth name was
Abdul Lateef Jandali.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.440
His father, Abdulfattah al Jandali was born
to a well-off Arab family in Syria.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.519
His mother Joanne Schieble, a Swiss-German
American,

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:46.489
was a graduate student at the University of
Wisconsin where they met.

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:49.940
She became pregnant when the two were visiting
Abdulfattah’s family in Syria,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.890
and she quickly high-tailed it back to California
to give birth to Steve.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:57.840
They decided to put the baby up for adoption
because they were not married.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.629
Initially, they chose a wealthy Catholic family.

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:02.050
But the adoption fell through,

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.590
and the child went to Paul and Clara Jobs.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.460
Paul was a mechanic, Clara an accountant.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.460
Neither

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.500
had gone to university.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.790
Before al Jandali would agree to the adoption,
the couple had to promise

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.710
that Steve would get a college education one
day.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.570
Steve’s biological parents did eventually
marry

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.890
and had another child, Steve’s sister.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.560
The successful novelist, Mona Simpson.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.700
She and Steve remained close until his death.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.770
But his relationship with his birth parents

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.070
was virtually non-existent.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.390
He told biographer Walter

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.719
Isaacson that his biological parents were
"They were my sperm and egg bank.

00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:38.330
That's not harsh, it's just the way it was.”

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.439
He did, however, choose to meet his biological

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:45.180
mother “...to thank her, because I’m glad
I didn’t end up as an abortion.”

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.300
In 1959, the Jobs family moved from San Francisco
to Mountain View, California.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.090
Mountain View was becoming a center for electronics.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:58.430
Young Steve began dabbling in electronics
but he was far less interested in school.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.850
A fourth-grade teacher tried to cure his boredom
by reportedly bribing him

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:06.399
with candy and $5 of her own bills to get
him to finish his schoolwork.

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:09.280
It apparently kindled a passion in him for
learning.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.020
So much so that he skipped fifth grade and
went directly to middle school.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.820
But life was hard at Crittenden Middle School
where he was often bullied.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:21.430
Jobs gave his parents an ultimatum: either
take him out or he would drop out.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.400
He ended up going to a better school district
when his parents moved to Los

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.750
Altos in the heart of the emerging Silicon
Valley.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.692
He attended Homestead high school where a
friend

00:02:29.692 --> 00:02:33.700
introduced him to Steve Wozniak who graduated
a few years earlier.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.690
They bonded over their love of electronics.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.350
Jobs would later found Apple with Wozniak.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:43.020
When he was still in high school, Jobs cold-called
Bill Hewlett of Hewlett-Packard

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.860
one day to ask for leftover electronic parts.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.400
He got the parts and a summer internship.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.340
Jobs worked on the assembly line at HP putting
nuts and bolts on frequency counters.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.470
After he graduated high school and went to
college, he mentioned his HP experience

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:01.870
on his application for a campus job - though
he misspelled Hewlett-Packard.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.940
This job application was not very inspiring.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.980
He vaguely lists his address as Reed College.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.450
Jobs famously dropped out of the liberal arts
school in Oregon after one semester

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.610
because he didn’t like the required classes.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.510
However, he later said his experiences at
Reed

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.040
helped him in everything that he’s ever
done.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.310
Because after dropping out,

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.680
he still hung around campus, dropping in on
the courses he found interesting,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.690
which would help shape the innovator he became.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.970
He took a class in calligraphy where he learned

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:36.960
about beautiful fonts and typefaces, about
varying spaces between letter combinations.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.030
It didn’t appear to have any practical application
at the time.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:45.370
But as Jobs would later say: “If I had never
dropped out, I would have never dropped in

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:46.370
on this

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.430
calligraphy class, and personal computers
might not have the wonderful typography that

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.430
they do.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:56.200
Of course, it was impossible to connect the
dots looking forward when I was in college.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.200
But it was

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.200
very, very clear looking backward 10 years
later.”

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.990
His days at college were otherwise spent recycling

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:06.280
Coca-Cola bottles for money for food and hitchhiking
across town to the local Hare

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.990
Krishna temple for a free Sunday meal.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:10.780
His diet would become very obsessive

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:11.780
later on.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.840
He’d eat nothing but fruit and leafy greens
which he said prevented his body from

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.300
forming harmful mucus.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.340
And he regularly fasted as a way to cleanse
his body.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.669
While at Reed, he also got a part-time gig
at the psychology department, r epairing equipment

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:28.740
used for experiments on pigeons and rats.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.740
When he wasn’t at work, he spent long hours

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.050
at the college library reading about Buddhism.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.460
He told biographer Isaacson that the simplicities
of the religion informed his sense of

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.150
design - apparent not only in Apple’s beautifully
simple designs but in his personal life.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.669
Jobs was famous for wearing simple black turtlenecks
and blue jeans.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:51.620
During his time in college, he also experimented
with psychedelics.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:57.020
He described using LSD as one of the most
profound experiences of his life,

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.900
crediting it with opening up his mind.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.960
And the name “Apple” came from visiting
an

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.360
apple farm with another acid tripper from
Reed.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.099
While at college, Wozniak paid him a visit.

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:09.400
He brought an early prototype of a hacking
device

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.600
that lets users make free international phone
calls.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.080
Before long, Steve and Woz were building blue

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.849
boxes themselves and selling them to their
friends which was highly illegal.

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:22.770
Making the blue boxes pushed the boundaries
of what they could do

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:26.780
with technology and would lead them further
down the path to Apple.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:31.300
But before then, Jobs started working at Atari,
the American video game developer.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.030
He was hired in 1974 as a technician after
he showed them a version of Pong

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.840
that they assumed he had created when in fact
Wozniak said it was his work.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.939
Jobs was difficult to get along with.

00:05:42.939 --> 00:05:45.020
But Atari co-founder Nolan Bushnell knew

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:50.280
Jobs was valuable, once remarking: “he was
very often the smartest guy in the room,

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.949
and he would let people know that.”

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:54.150
Jobs’ prickly personality and lack

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.930
of hygiene are said to have been the reasons
he got put on the night shift.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:02.520
He used to soak his feet in toilet water as
a stress reliever when he worked at Apple.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.319
But in a Reddit thread, Bushnell mentioned
he got Jobs to work overnight because he knew

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:11.199
Wozniak would visit him since they were buddies,
so: “I'd get two Steves for the price of

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:12.199
one.”

00:06:12.199 --> 00:06:16.160
It was thanks to Atari that Jobs fulfilled
his desire to travel to India.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.040
When Atari sent Jobs on a trip to Germany,
he convinced the company to give him a one-way

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.040
ticket

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.909
and the rest, he’d take in cash.

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:26.280
Then, instead of flying back to California,
he flew to India.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:30.400
He wanted to go on a spiritual retreat to
find his inner self.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.990
His travel companion was Daniel Kottke, a
college friend and

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.469
later one of the first employees of Apple.

00:06:36.469 --> 00:06:39.639
India was a life-changing experience for Jobs.

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:42.590
He soaked in the culture, wore loose-fitting
clothing,

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:47.560
and roamed around barefoot - a habit he would
later continue around Apple’s offices.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:52.469
He embraced eastern spirituality,
including the precept of intuition.

00:06:52.469 --> 00:06:55.990
When he traveled to the Indian countryside,
he noticed that people didn’t use their

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:00.290
intellect like Americans did, but instead
relied more on intuition.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:04.460
He told biographer Isaacson: "Intuition is
a very powerful thing,

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.900
more powerful than intellect, in my opinion.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.860
That's had a big impact on my work."

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.719
It was intuition that helped him understand
what customers wanted

00:07:12.719 --> 00:07:15.539
when he went on to develop Apple’s products.

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:18.059
He returned home from India with a shaved
head and

00:07:18.059 --> 00:07:23.219
a powerful intuition that prompted him to
start Apple with Steve Wozniak and Ronald

00:07:23.219 --> 00:07:25.750
Wayne in 1976.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:29.389
Wayne acted more like the adult supervisor
to the younger Wozniak and Jobs,

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:30.389
who were still in their twenties.

00:07:30.389 --> 00:07:32.979
But he left after less than two weeks,

00:07:32.979 --> 00:07:36.309
selling his 10% stake for $800.

00:07:36.309 --> 00:07:39.319
That - by the way - is worth billions today!

00:07:39.319 --> 00:07:42.430
The company’s headquarters was the garage
of Jobs’ parents.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:43.879
That’s where they built

00:07:43.879 --> 00:07:48.699
Apple’s first computer, the Apple 1 with
the help of Jobs’ India travel buddy, Kottke.

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:51.020
It wasn’t anything like the computers of
today.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:53.410
It had no keyboard or screen and served more

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:57.719
as part of a kit that computer hobbyists could
build into something more useful.

00:07:57.719 --> 00:08:01.599
The owner of this unit added a keyboard and
case afterward.

00:08:01.599 --> 00:08:05.759
Woz coded while Jobs spent hours on the phone
trying to find investors.

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:06.759
He managed to convince

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:12.509
a former Intel executive to put up some cash,
allowing them to produce 200 Apple Ones.

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:16.300
Wozniak continued to mold it into something
better and the following year,

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:20.610
they unveiled the Apple II - a personal computer
aimed at the consumer market.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.860
It could be taken home, plugged in, and used.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.779
The Apple II became the symbol of the

00:08:25.779 --> 00:08:28.129
personal computing revolution.

00:08:28.129 --> 00:08:29.449
Over the next three years,

00:08:29.449 --> 00:08:38.510
Apple’s annual sales ballooned from $775,000
to $118 million and so did Jobs’ net worth.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.930
But not everything the company did was a hit.

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:43.640
The Apple III and Lisa were flops.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:48.360
The Lisa computer, launched in 1983, was named
after Jobs’ child.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:52.290
He wasn’t happy when his high school sweetheart
Chrisann Brennan became pregnant

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.800
and denied he was the father for years.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.870
He refused to admit the Apple computer was

00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:01.430
named after Lisa for the longest time and
chalked it up to coincidence, saying LISA

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:02.490
stood for Local

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.460
Integrated Software Architecture.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.630
A DNA test proved he was the father.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:12.830
Decades later, he confessed to Isaacson:
“Obviously, it was named for my daughter.”

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:17.970
He may have changed the world but Jobs failed
in ordinary ways, like being a father to Lisa.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.540
They had a strained relationship.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.990
Lisa would later say, ‘Clearly I was not
compelling enough for my father'.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:29.040
As for the computer that carried her name,
the failure would be forgotten when Apple

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:34.590
launched the Macintosh in 1984 during the
Super Bowl in this now-famous commercial.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:38.840
On January 24th, Apple Computer will introduce
Macintosh.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:39.840
And

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.440
you’ll see why 1984 won’t be like 1984.”

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.760
That’s a reference to George Orwell’s

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.400
famous dystopian novel.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.530
The estate of Orwell sent a cease-and-desist
order

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.030
to Apple and the commercial never aired again.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:55.440
And, as it turned out, the Mac would cost
Jobs

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.220
more than public embarrassment.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:59.680
It would cost him his job.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.200
He was in charge of the MacIntosh division
and when the expensive original version didn’t

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.200
sell

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:10.350
as well as he expected, he told CEO John Scully
to lower the price and spend more on advertising.

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:13.950
Scully didn’t think it had anything to do
with the price or advertising.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:18.270
He went to Apple’s Board of Directors, which
sided with him, and got Jobs ousted as head

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:19.270
of

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:22.700
the Macintosh division and stripped him of
all of his executive duties.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.560
Jobs was forced out of Apple.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:26.890
When he resigned, he took five employees with
him

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.820
to form a new company.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:29.820
NeXT specialized in

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.760
computers aimed at the higher education market.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.330
In 1986, he also invested some of his wealth
by

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:40.100
acquiring the computer graphics division of
George Lucas’ special effects company, renamed

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.150
Pixar.

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:45.460
He later sold it to Disney for $7.4 billion
in stock.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:50.540
He was Pixar’s CEO when it produced Toy
Story, the first feature-length film

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:51.680
entirely created in 3D animation.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:55.460
The main character, Woody, had evolved into
a jerk,

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:58.240
mainly because of script changes by Disney.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.580
Jobs fought back to make Woody into a likable

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:01.580
character.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:05.950
This demonstrated his ability to rework something
that he thought wasn’t perfect.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:10.230
He told Issacson, "If something isn't right,
you can't just ignore it and say you'll fix

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:11.230
it later.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:13.110
That’s what other companies do."

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:14.760
Unfortunately, Jobs didn’t take this

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.760
to heart when it came to his own health, which
worsened when he took on more work.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:25.720
At the time that Toy Story became a monumental
success, Apple was in a financial mess.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:31.360
In 1997, it was 90 days away from bankruptcy
because it failed to innovate.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:36.430
Apple desperately needed a new, modern operating
system and in a remarkable turn of events,

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:37.430
bought

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:38.830
NeXT for $400 million.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:44.450
The NeXT operating system paved the way for
the OS X - later renamed MacOS.

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:48.930
Jobs returned to Apple as CEO, while continuing
to lead Pixar.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:53.530
With Jobs in the driver’s seat, Apple was
about to go in a different direction.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:57.600
He worked closely with designer Jony Ive to
develop stunning new products:

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:01.360
the iMac, iPod, iPhone, and iPad.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.950
Apple wanted to let the world know it was
different from other companies.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.320
It came up with the “Think Different”
advertising campaign in response to IBM’s

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:10.680
“Think” slogan.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:11.680
IBM

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.140
was at the time considered the top of the
PC line.

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.880
“Think Different” doesn’t make sense
grammatically

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:21.210
but Jobs insisted “Think Differently”
would not have the same effect.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.090
This is one of the Think Different commercials.

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:25.600
"Here’s to the crazy ones."

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.120
This commercial was crucial in turning around
Apple.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.860
The original version was actually voiced by
Jobs.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.360
"The round pegs in the square holes."

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.060
It never aired because he hated his version
and let it be known.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:42.410
There was a side of him that could be nasty
according to those who worked with him.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:46.100
Rob Siltanen, a creative director on the Think
Different campaign,

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.000
later wrote in a Forbes Magazine article,

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.720
“I must say I saw and experienced his tongue
lashings and ballistic temper firsthand.”

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:57.560
Jobs attributed his angry outbursts to perfectionism.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:01.050
He was such a perfectionist he had trouble
buying furniture - his home

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:03.880
was only furnished with the bare essentials.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.240
His temper was in sharp contrast to his public

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.100
image as a warm and loving family man.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:10.890
He met his wife, Laurene Powell when

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:15.760
she was an MBA student at Stanford where he
gave a lecture in 1989.

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:19.180
They married two years later and had three
children together.

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:23.250
Despite his difficult personality, employees
concede that there’s no way

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:27.210
Apple could have completely turned around
its business without Jobs.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.290
And his genius continued to flourish.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:32.230
"There it is, right there."

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:36.480
In 2001, Apple introduced the iPod which changed
not only how we listen

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.440
to music but the entire music industry.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.290
And in 2007, he took to the stage to announce

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:45.780
the most successful product launch to date.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:52.510
“An iPod, a phone, and an Internet communicator.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:14:01.790
An iPod, a phone…are you getting it?

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:07.220
These are not 3 separate devices.

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:11.040
This is one device,

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:16.780
and we are calling it iPhone.”

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:23.910
The future seemed without limits, but Jobs
had already begun to have health problems.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:28.760
He traced it back to the brutal demands of
running two companies at the same time,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:33.590
telling Isaacson, “It was rough, really
rough, the worst time in my life.

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:34.810
I had a young family.

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:35.940
I had Pixar.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:40.930
I would go to work at 7 a.m. and I'd get back
at 9 at night, and the kids would be in

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:42.410
bed.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.570
And I couldn’t speak, I literally couldn't,
I was so exhausted.

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:46.490
I couldn't speak to Laurene.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:49.310
All I could do was watch a half-hour of TV
and vegetate.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.180
It got close to killing me.”

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:53.770
He started to get kidney stones.

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:59.130
When Jobs had a cat scan for kidney stones
one day in 2003, doctors saw a shadow on his

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:00.130
pancreas.

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:04.550
It turned out to be a neuroendocrine tumor,
a very rare form of pancreatic

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:07.630
cancer that is often treatable with surgery.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.230
Unlike the common form of pancreatic cancer,

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:13.530
survival is measured in years if not decades.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.670
But according to Isaacson, he refused to

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:21.610
undergo surgery for nine months, and later
regretted his decision as his health declined.

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:26.900
Jobs initially opted for alternative treatments
such as acupuncture, dietary supplements,

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:27.900
and

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.950
juices instead of potentially life-saving
surgery.

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:32.010
Why?

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.700
Because he thought he could bend reality to
his will.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.770
It worked for him at Apple.

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:40.950
Employees often joked that Jobs would distort
reality to achieve his goals.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:41.950
Like the time when

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:47.000
he asked Apple engineer Larry Kenyon to reduce
the boot time of the Macintosh by 10 seconds.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:51.470
Kenyon said it was impossible, but Jobs didn’t
care, telling him to find a way.

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:56.130
As Isaacson recounted, Jobs said: "If it could
save a person's life, would you find a way

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:57.130
to

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.730
shave ten seconds off the boot time?"

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:00.450
A few weeks later, Kenyon reduced

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:03.150
the boot time by 28 seconds.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:06.420
So Jobs willed the impossible possible.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:10.010
In the same way, when it came to his health,
he ignored reality

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:13.010
and wanted to bend reality to his liking.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.860
Isaacson gave his thoughts to CBS:

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:19.430
"I think that he kind of felt that if you
ignore something, if you don't want something

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:20.430
to exist,

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:21.700
you can have magical thinking.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.300
And it had worked for him in the past.”

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:28.061
As his condition worsened, he had part of
his pancreas removed which bought him some

00:16:28.061 --> 00:16:29.061
time

00:16:29.061 --> 00:16:31.560
but wasn’t enough to eradicate the disease.

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:34.250
In January 2009, he took a six-month medical
leave

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:36.900
of absence to focus on his health.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:39.240
That April, he had a liver transplant.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:45.090
Tim Cook became acting CEO of Apple, with
Jobs still involved with major strategic decisions.

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:48.430
For the next two years, he continued working
off and on.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:54.820
Then on August 24, 2011, he resigned as CEO
in this letter made public on Apple’s website:

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:59.460
"I have always said if there ever came a day
when I could no longer meet my duties and

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:00.460
expectations

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:04.040
as Apple's CEO, I would be the first to let
you know.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:07.409
Unfortunately, that day has come."

00:17:07.409 --> 00:17:10.569
Jobs became chairman of the Board and continued
to work for Apple

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:13.449
reportedly until the day before he died.

00:17:13.449 --> 00:17:17.939
On October 5, 2011, around 3pm Pacific time,

00:17:17.939 --> 00:17:22.589
he passed away surrounded by his family at
his Palo Alto home.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:27.179
He was 56 years old.

00:17:27.179 --> 00:17:32.330
Jobs once said: Death is very likely the single
best invention of Life.

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.509
It is Life’s change agent.

00:17:34.509 --> 00:17:37.409
It clears out the old to make way for the
new.

00:17:37.409 --> 00:17:38.840
Right now the new is you,

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:42.200
but someday not too long from now, you will
gradually become

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.970
the old and be cleared away.

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:47.970
Sorry to be so dramatic, but it is quite true.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:49.659
Jobs had a small, private funeral

00:17:49.659 --> 00:17:53.570
but he was mourned around the world like the
passing of a rock star.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:57.659
He was buried in an unmarked grave - at his
own request - at Alta Mesa Memorial

00:17:57.659 --> 00:18:00.529
Park in the heart of Silicon Valley.

00:18:00.529 --> 00:18:02.169
Reflecting on his life, when he had

00:18:02.169 --> 00:18:07.190
to build his way back up again after getting
fired from the company he started, he said:

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:12.039
“I'm convinced that the only thing that
kept me going was that I loved what I did.

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:13.039
You've

00:18:13.039 --> 00:18:14.249
got to find what you love.

00:18:14.249 --> 00:18:18.289
And that is as true for your work as it is
for your lovers.

00:18:18.289 --> 00:18:23.509
Your work is going to fill a large part of
your life, and the only way to be truly satisfied

00:18:23.509 --> 00:18:26.470
is to do what you believe is great work.

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:28.409
And the only way to do great work

00:18:28.409 --> 00:18:30.610
is to love what you do.

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:32.300
If you haven't found it yet,

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:33.300
keep looking.

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:34.429
Don't settle.

00:18:34.429 --> 00:18:38.740
As with all matters of the heart, you'll know
when you find it.

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:43.639
Steve Jobs believed that his intuition and
curiosity would guide him down the right path.

00:18:43.639 --> 00:18:48.759
If you’re curious about the world and want
to learn more, my sponsor Curiosity Stream

